WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.175
So far, you have
learned how to use

2
00:00:02.175 --> 00:00:05.760
SQL arithmetic operators to
perform basic functions.

3
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.350
In this video, you'll take

4
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.060
one step further
by learning how to

5
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.530
use these arithmetic
operators in

6
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.720
practice when working
with a database.

7
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.585
Let's explore the use
of arithmetic operators

8
00:00:15.585 --> 00:00:19.110
using the example of a corporate
table of employee data.

9
00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.870
It's populated with the
following information about

10
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.710
each employee: ID,
name, and salary.

11
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.230
I'm going to demonstrate
how to use each

12
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.330
of the arithmetic
operators in SQL to

13
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.070
perform various functions with

14
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.820
this practical data and
retrieve the desired results.

15
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.835
In this first example,

16
00:00:37.835 --> 00:00:40.295
let's say that an
employer wants to give

17
00:00:40.295 --> 00:00:44.450
each employee on their
team a $500 bonus.

18
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.550
But first, they'd
like to see what

19
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.810
each employee's salary looks
like after adding the $500.

20
00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:59.930
I can type SELECT salary plus

21
00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:05.210
500 FROM employee to

22
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:09.540
add the $500 bonus to
each employee's salary.

23
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.900
The SELECT command works by

24
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.820
retrieving the salary
of each employee from

25
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.845
the employee table and then
adding $500 to each value.

26
00:01:17.845 --> 00:01:21.505
Let's execute the query by
clicking the go button.

27
00:01:21.505 --> 00:01:24.575
The result is that
each employee's salary

28
00:01:24.575 --> 00:01:26.210
has increased by 500.

29
00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.015
You can create other statements

30
00:01:29.015 --> 00:01:31.700
that use the SELECT
command in a similar way.

31
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.970
For example, let's say
the employer wants

32
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.720
to deduct $500 from the
salary of each employee.

33
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.500
To do this, I typed the
SQL query statement.

34
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:48.420
SELECT salary minus
500 from employee.

35
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.190
Again, I'm using the
select command to retrieve

36
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.215
the salary value of
each employee record

37
00:01:54.215 --> 00:01:56.170
stored in the employee table.

38
00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:59.225
This time, I used
a subtraction sign

39
00:01:59.225 --> 00:02:03.560
minus subtract $500 from the
salary of each employee.

40
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.360
Now I click the Go button

41
00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.060
and the database returns
a table that shows

42
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.920
the output result of salaries
after the deductions.

43
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.025
In the next example,

44
00:02:17.025 --> 00:02:18.650
let's imagine a scenario where

45
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.330
the employer would
like to increase

46
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.100
the employee's salaries by

47
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.925
doubling their current
annual salary.

48
00:02:24.925 --> 00:02:27.920
To do this, I use the
SELECT command to

49
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.755
perform the same function
as discussed previously.

50
00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:34.775
I then use the
multiplication sign,

51
00:02:34.775 --> 00:02:37.160
an asterisk symbol to

52
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.940
multiply the salary value
of each employee by two.

53
00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.970
The SQL statement now reads

54
00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.885
SELECT salary asterisks
two from employee.

55
00:02:52.885 --> 00:02:55.640
I can now click the Go button
to generate an output of

56
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.020
the results of salaries from

57
00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.520
multiplying the values by two.

58
00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.450
Now let's suppose the
employer needs to

59
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.225
determine the monthly
salary of each employee.

60
00:03:05.225 --> 00:03:07.160
I can perform this task using

61
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.600
an SQL query statement

62
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.675
as I have done in the
previous scenarios.

63
00:03:10.675 --> 00:03:12.770
However, this time I'll

64
00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.860
divide the annual
salary by 12 months.

65
00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.735
To determine the monthly salary.

66
00:03:17.735 --> 00:03:20.540
My statement reads as follows.

67
00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:32.365
SELECT salary forward
slash 12 FROM employee.

68
00:03:32.365 --> 00:03:34.100
The select command retrieves

69
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.780
the salary value
for each employee

70
00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.340
stored in the employee table.

71
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.230
Then division sign or

72
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.920
the forward slash divides the
annual salary value by 12.

73
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.310
I click the Go button to
generate the output results.

74
00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:51.395
The employer can now
use this output result

75
00:03:51.395 --> 00:03:55.050
to determine what each
employee's monthly salary is.

76
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.410
In our final example,

77
00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.660
the employer would like
to know if the ID of

78
00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.840
each employee is an
even or odd number.

79
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.815
I can use the modulus operator
to complete this task.

80
00:04:06.815 --> 00:04:10.320
I just need to type the
following statement.

81
00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:21.110
SELECT ID modulus

82
00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:26.080
two from employee.

83
00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.595
I then click Go to
execute the statement.

84
00:04:29.595 --> 00:04:32.630
This divides each employee ID by

85
00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:36.235
two and returns the remainder
of the division operation.

86
00:04:36.235 --> 00:04:39.200
In this case, a remainder
of 0 denotes an

87
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.650
even id as all even numbers
are divisible by two,

88
00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.230
meaning there will
be no remainder.

89
00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.225
The result shows that there are

90
00:04:47.225 --> 00:04:51.455
three odd IDs and to even IDs.

91
00:04:51.455 --> 00:04:53.540
You should now know
how to make use of

92
00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.225
the SQL arithmetic
operators in a database.

93
00:04:56.225 --> 00:04:59.800
You're making great
progress. Keep it up.